    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i also saw a section about how blockages can mess up the readings. what kind of stuff usually clogs them up and how does that affect the instruments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Blockages in the pitot tube can be a real nuisance, and it's essential to understand what can cause them. The pitot tube is prone to clogging from ice, snow, or even a bit of dirt or debris that gets sucked in during takeoff or landing. It's like a tiny goblin's lair in there, waiting to trap unsuspecting air molecules and cause trouble.\n\nWhen the pitot tube becomes clogged, it can create a false airspeed reading, which can lead to a range of problems. For example, if the pitot tube is clogged with ice, the aircraft may be reading a higher airspeed than it actually is, which can cause the autopilot system to become confused and make incorrect adjustments. On the other hand, if the pitot tube is clogged with debris, the aircraft may be reading a lower airspeed than it actually is, which can cause the pilot to overcorrect and lose altitude.\n\nIn extreme cases, a clogged pitot tube can even cause the aircraft to stall, which can be catastrophic. It's like a tiny goblin has taken control of the aircraft's airspeed, and it's up to the pilot to figure out how to get it back on track.",
      "source": "target",
